  "response": "Isotopic rate measurements supply the complementary probe. In the reaction under scrutiny the measured kH/kD value was 1.0 within experimental error; in other words, replacing the hydrogen at the reacting position with deuterium produced no change in the reaction rate whatsoever. The unit ratio indicates that complete stereospecific transfer of substrate geometry into the product is consistent with a concerted pathway, since the geometric fidelity of the transformation is then not purchased at the cost of a hidden rate-limiting proton transfer. Taken with the product ratios, the isotopic result fixes which of the candidate surfaces the synthetic chemist should adopt in planning the next substrate.\n\nCrossover experiments combine two differently labelled substrates and search the products for scrambled label combinations, and the logic of that pairing establishes that formation of crossover products shows fragments to have become separated during the course of the reaction. A strictly concerted reaction run with two labelled substrates delivers crossover products, and that behaviour cannot be squared with treating scrambled label combinations as a mark of fragments having come apart; one of the two accounts of crossover must be abandoned. Practitioners therefore design the labelling pattern so that the diagnostic reading of the product mixture is not left hostage to whichever account is preferred, and they repeat the experiment at several conversions.\n\nThe question is then posed as a strict choice about topography. Either the potential energy surface for the reaction contains no intermediate between reactants and products, or that surface contains at least one intermediate between reactants and products; one of these must hold of the reaction, and no single surface can answer to both. The anomalous product distribution admits a comparably disciplined reading: at least one of two things is the case, the two products being formed at unequal rates, or the two products interconverting after they are formed, and both may well be true together. As a matter of general method, primary kinetic isotope effects report on bonds broken or formed at the rate-determining transition state.\n\nThe adjudication turns first on a structural distinction that must be stated precisely. A concerted mechanism proceeds through a single transition state with no intervening intermediate, whereas a stepwise mechanism contains a discrete intermediate that occupies a local minimum on the potential energy surface. Intermediates appear as first-order saddle points on that surface, a characterization that cannot be squared with residence in a local minimum, so the two descriptions of what an intermediate is cannot both be sustained, and any mechanist weighing an unexpected product ratio has to decide which description governs the analysis.",
